# One game's crank settings. The provisioner creates /etc/<game>/crank.env from this file when it
# is missing, unless the game ships its own <game>.env.example, which it uses instead; by hand:
#
#     install -d -m 0700 /etc/<game>
#     cp crank.env.example /etc/<game>/crank.env
#     systemctl restart <game>-crank
#
# Each game has its own file; nothing here is shared between games.
# An update replaces the code and the unit file; it never touches this file.
# Anything set here overrides the unit's shipped defaults. Everything is
# optional — with none of it the bot still runs, and tells nobody when it dies.
#
# There is no payee section: this bot earns nothing (the resolve fee goes to
# SPT's treasury inside the contract), so there is nothing to redirect and its
# account only ever holds gas.

# KNOWING WHEN IT DIES ---------------------------------------------------------
# A ping URL from any service that alerts when pings STOP — several hosted
# uptime monitors have a free tier, or run your own. The bot pings after every pass in which
# it read the chain, so silence means this machine, its network or its node is
# gone, and you hear it from somewhere that did not die with it.
#
# It proves the bot is alive and reading the chain. It does not prove a given
# round settled. Nothing here is secret to the bot, but the URL is a capability:
# anyone with it can fake your heartbeat, so keep this file to mode 0600 if you
# set one. This is the one setting most people set.
#HEARTBEAT_URL=https://<your monitoring service>/<your check's ping id>

# THE NODE ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The unit already points at the live table on mainnet chain 2 through
# chainweb.eckowallet.com. Another public node, for the second crank or as a
# fallback when the first is down:
#CRANK_HOST=https://api.chainweb-community.org
# game-crank.mjs reads CRANK_* only. roulette-crank.mjs, the single-game crank
# it replaces, read the same settings as ROULETTE_HOST and so on; the game
# provisioner refuses a file that still sets those, rather than let a setting
# be ignored without a word.

# THE MODULE -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The unit names the production module, n_48867b242317a0216a67f8c7ca26696b5878e0e3.<game>.
# Set this only when a game's module has a different name:
#CRANK_MOD=n_48867b242317a0216a67f8c7ca26696b5878e0e3.<game>

# PACE -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LIVENESS_MS is how old the ON-CHAIN liveness record may get before the bot
# refreshes it. The default is six hours, ~360x inside the module's 90-day void
# window; shorter buys nothing but gas.
#LIVENESS_MS=21600000

# CRANK_FOLLOWER_MS makes this bot the FOLLOWER of a pair: it waits this long after a round becomes
# actionable (beacon published, payout owed, liveness due) before its dry run, so the leader's send
# has landed and nothing is sent twice. 0 (the default) is the leader. Set 90000 on exactly one
# machine of each pair: a lost race pays the loser's whole gas limit, and a delay shorter than a
# block interval (30 s mean, 136 s longest measured) changes nothing.
#CRANK_FOLLOWER_MS=90000

# CLAIMS_PER_TX is how many winners are paid in one transaction. 40 is the
# measured shape; the bot caps whatever you set at 120, which is still well
# inside the gas ceiling at the measured 445 gas a claim. What has NOT been
# verified is chainweb's per-command size limit above 40 claims: raise this only
# after a batch of that size has actually landed on a node.
#CLAIMS_PER_TX=40
